Z||000033||RiceCooker||12-08-2000||03:38 PM||||Shortly after I got my car last year, my dad and I took a drive to Montana for the day. I live in Alberta so it was about 4 hours just to get to the border. I got it to the end of the engine. 86 SE 4 spd. The needle was at the end and we sped up a bit more so I'd guess about 135. This is not safe with stock tires. There was a mountain pass almost at Helena about 15 miles long. We went back and forth through there taking turns between 80-100 miles an hour - OMG. What a rush!!!<P>I'd love to do it again now since I have 40series Z rated Pirellis and a slightly modified engine. Only thing is, I need a larger speedo.||||reg||1
Z||000034||CUTTER||12-09-2000||02:33 AM||||125 in the fiero 87GT<BR>150 in my 91mr2 turbo||||reg||1
Z||000035||Eric_PA||12-09-2000||10:26 AM||||I don't know why I decided to post my speed record numbers, but what the heck. Everyone else is. The higest speed I had my automatic GT up to was 115-120MPH. I stoped going faster when the headlight covers started to flap in the wind. But in my 97 Sunfire GT I have had that up to a little over 130MPH. You will ask how did you overcome the speed limiter, easy you bypass the speed sending unit and make the computer think it is still going 100 or so. That way the car doesn't shut down on you. by the way I tried going this fast with the stock clutch and it let go on me. I ended up replacing it this fall with a Centerforce Dual Friction. Still not happy with this clutch. I took the sunfire to the track recently and ran a best of 15.782 @ 87.5 MPH. not bad for a 4 cylynder. cant wait to get the quad into the GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000036||Songman||12-09-2000||01:06 PM||||Not sure in a Feiro... probably around 100.<P>Always took all my others to around 140 just to be sure they would do it. These included a v12 Jag XJS, a 911 Targa, and a Corvette.<P>I found out quickly that 140 is really faster than I care to go....<P>Had my Harley up to about 85 passing on the interstate once... Also found that I don't care for much over 65-70 on it!||||reg||1
Z||000037||KnightRyder31||12-09-2000||02:49 PM||||Lets see...<BR>1989 Bonneville SSEI(Modded)-145-150MPH.<BR>1984 Fiero- Speedo was almost at 0.<BR>1985 Fiero GT- Speedo was pegged out<BR>1994 Talon TSI- 140MPH<BR>1987 911- 140MPH<BR>1998 Firehawk(WS6 Pack)-140MPH<BR>1970 Challenger R/T 426HEMI- 160MPH<BR>1977 Trans Am 455-130MPH<BR>2000 Grand Am SE- 113MPH<BR>1964 GTO- 130MPH<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||StuGood||12-09-2000||03:49 PM||||Time to confess.  Sometime after I bought my 1986 SE 4-speed V6, I decided I wanted to see how fast it would go.  The opportunity came along one summer night (1986 or 1987, probably), driving alone on a long, straight, lonely Kansas road in the wee hours.<P>Headlights were up (I'm not <B>that</B> crazy), pretty sure turned the A/C off, for the "maximum speed" test.  Speedo only went to 85mph - needle bounced off the peg for quite a little while, as the car continued to accelerate.<P>Finally reached "terminal velocity."  It was definitely <B>not</B> the rev limiter- the engine just didn't have enough "oomph" to push the car any faster.  Top speed was drag-limited, in other words.  Probably thought about dropping the headlights for a sec, to see if it'd do any better - then thought again.<P>As others mentioned, the car was very quiet and stable at that speed - kind of spooky how relaxed I felt about the whole thing.  Noted the tach reading, then slowed down to a saner speed. <P>Later, I ratioed the tach reading in 4th gear at 55mph (2250, I think?), to the tach reading at "terminal velocity," and came up with about 132 mph.  Next, looked up the 4th gear and final drive ratios, measured the rear tire outside diameter, and calculated the same thing, within a few percent.  <P>Oreif, found it interesting that you came out with the same top speed on a similar car.  Makes me think I probably calculated it correctly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Don't remember now where I put the numbers (probably on a napkin, envelope, etc. - put away somewhere). <P>When people ask me how fast my Fiero can go, I usually say, "Something between 125 and 135 mph."  I figure that's a pretty safe, honest answer.||||reg||6
Z||000039||Seanh||12-09-2000||09:33 PM||||I have had the GT up to about 115, it was a solid ride and great handling. Fastest I have ever gone was 152 in my boss's new Honda S2000. He ordered this car over a year ago and just now got it. Its a white convertable, I hear the convertables are not very common. Wow, that thing can haul, but the control mod. cuts it off at 160, it could go faster.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I wont do that again until I have some dipers handy.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000040||Spyder||12-10-2000||12:12 AM||||Fiero, pegged out, no clue top speed.<BR>Fastest: 150 Nissan 300zx.<BR>A lesson Learned:  <BR>Note to self, 130 on a two lane, don’t meet a 18 wheeler going 70.  The car shook violently, windshield wipers flew up to straight up, the pressure made windshield wiper fluid piss on the windshield. Kind of scary. ||||reg||1
